WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.571
Zero Trust is a security strategy, not a single product, feature, or vendor service.

00:00:06.571 --> 00:00:08.420
Technology enables the strategy,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:14.891
but governance and operating processes determine how access decisions support business and risk objectives.

00:00:14.891 --> 00:00:17.202
Leadership establishes direction and accountability.

00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:22.287
Executives and risk owners define priorities, approve investment, resolve cross-team barriers,

00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:28.296
and decide how the organization balances security, user experience, cost, and mission needs.

00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:30.607
Governance translates strategy into policy.

00:00:30.607 --> 00:00:35.691
It defines protected resources, identity and device expectations, least privilege principles,

00:00:35.691 --> 00:00:39.851
monitoring requirements, exceptions, metrics, review cycles, and accountable owners.

00:00:39.851 --> 00:00:42.624
Architecture connects those policies to systems.

00:00:42.624 --> 00:00:46.322
Identity platforms, endpoint controls, applications, networks, data services,

00:00:46.322 --> 00:00:52.793
and monitoring tools need consistent information and enforcement paths rather than isolated product decisions.

00:00:52.793 --> 00:00:55.567
Operational processes keep the model working.

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:59.264
Access requests, employee changes, device enrollment, application onboarding,

00:00:59.264 --> 00:01:02.038
incident response, exception review, vendor access,

00:01:02.038 --> 00:01:07.122
and decommissioning all affect whether Zero Trust remains accurate over time.

00:01:07.122 --> 00:01:09.896
People are part of the design.

00:01:09.896 --> 00:01:13.593
Employees and administrators need clear expectations, usable authentication,

00:01:13.593 --> 00:01:16.829
understandable access requests, support when controls fail,

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:20.527
and training that explains why changes are occurring.

00:01:20.527 --> 00:01:26.536
Collaboration is essential because no single team owns every signal or enforcement point.

00:01:26.536 --> 00:01:31.158
Security, identity, network, endpoint, application, data, privacy, compliance, human resources,

00:01:31.158 --> 00:01:33.931
procurement, and business owners all contribute.

00:01:33.931 --> 00:01:37.167
A product purchase may improve one capability,

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:41.327
but it does not create Zero Trust by itself.

00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:45.024
The strategy succeeds when technology, policy, processes, ownership,

00:01:45.024 --> 00:01:48.260
and measurement produce consistent risk-informed access decisions.
